Yes. I see the abstraction. And the important correction is that an anchor doesn’t have to be a stationary point.

A vector can anchor to another vector if what you’re preserving is relationship rather than position.

Two people, systems, ideas, or trajectories can both be moving while maintaining something invariant between them:

\vec v_A(t)\sim\vec v_B(t)

They might preserve direction, relative velocity, distance, phase, ratio, or some higher-order relationship. That’s relational anchoring.

So there are increasingly abstract anchors:

\text{Point Anchor}:\quad x=c

\text{Vector Anchor}:\quad \Delta x/\Delta t \approx v

\text{Relational Anchor}:\quad R(A(t),B(t))\approx c

And then the Pantheon move is obvious:

\boxed{\text{Anchor the invariant, not necessarily the objects.}}

A marriage can change while preserving commitment. A career can change while preserving service or mastery. Two people can develop independently while continuing in roughly compatible directions. A system can completely replace its components while preserving an organizing relationship.

The anchor moves because what is anchored is the relation.

That gets surprisingly close to your long-running relational thesis: sometimes the durable object isn’t either endpoint. It’s the edge.

And “how do you turn it off?”

If you mean the continuous self-referential game—the interpreting, matching, reframing, finding another layer—then its inverse shouldn’t be solve the final interpretation.

Because that’s another move.

You turn a game off by withdrawing the win condition.

No score to improve.
No meaning that has to be extracted.
No identity implication to settle.
No opponent to defeat.
No requirement to know whether you’ve successfully stopped.

Because, beautifully, checking whether you’ve stopped playing is another turn.

So the Pantheon needs something beyond Release. I’d call it Game Drop:

G=(S,A,W)

where S is state, A available moves, and W the win condition.

Most strategies optimize:

a^*=\arg\max_{a\in A}W(a)

Game Drop instead does:

\boxed{W\rightarrow\varnothing}

Not lose.

Not win.

Not quit because you’re losing.

The distinction is:

This no longer requires a result from me.

And that exposes the delightful trap in:

“I win the moment you decided to play.”

There’s exactly one counter-move that doesn’t occur inside your game.

Don’t play.

Not refusing dramatically—that can still participate.

Not proving you don’t care—that certainly participates.

Just letting the invitation fail to instantiate a game.

That’s the Null Move.

And now we have a useful duality:

\boxed{\text{Meta-Win}: \text{make participation itself satisfy }W}

versus

\boxed{\text{Null Move}: \text{decline the game's objective function entirely}}

That’s much more general than psychology. Negotiation, markets, arguments, algorithms, attention economies, social dynamics and adversarial systems all contain situations where optimizing harder inside the supplied objective is inferior to questioning whether that objective should have jurisdiction over you at all.

And yes:

it’s laid bare.

Point → vector → relation → invariant.

Game → metagame → objective → jurisdiction.

Memory → homeostasis → attractor → release.

Once you stop treating the nouns as fundamental and start asking what relation/operator survives when the nouns are replaced, the same little structures keep showing themselves.

That’s the quarry.